#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ecf1ca4-abf8-5d39-acd9-71c36b940180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y^{′} + 9y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146780} {:answer/id #uuid "bf17ecc9-58fc-5b6b-ab91-d3c0574d59db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y^{′} + 18y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146781} {:answer/id #uuid "e7925941-31f9-55f9-aa8e-a49229b277f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 3y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146782} {:answer/id #uuid "5253d024-9512-5d29-9b97-8ea08fa7e649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 9y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146783} {:answer/id #uuid "795e15e9-0d25-536b-a1a4-2a1ede1b45a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y^{′} + 3y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ecf1ca4-abf8-5d39-acd9-71c36b940180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 5y^{′} + 9y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146780} :answer-field/id #uuid "9be58223-0c2f-5366-ba76-678ca17f6741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e5d3139-8000-5f12-824c-92daaf2b4fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-64593" :question/problem "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=10\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-10{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "817c9dd1-405f-50c6-b224-07e1ed1871f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146792} {:answer/id #uuid "977db0e1-760b-5b28-af49-24150ecef024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146793} {:answer/id #uuid "17307ad6-6ec6-5c5d-bfd4-61c99cd1945e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146794} {:answer/id #uuid "4372e6ea-b134-5d3e-a0ab-237bed9773fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146795} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad2c027-9b8b-57b3-aa4d-0eb0fd748a26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "977db0e1-760b-5b28-af49-24150ecef024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146793} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed63b8b3-c9a6-5747-93f2-be853fe37e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a95c4f-6f0b-5fe1-b32c-ae9f75fde633" :question/math-academy-id "q-64617" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcb1a67-db67-56b6-8cf2-26895aa25d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373359} {:answer/id #uuid "57dd3934-9bc5-54e5-9f11-0a86ef953b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373360} {:answer/id #uuid "025f9c8c-b92a-5b9d-9560-12d7e07a2303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373361} {:answer/id #uuid "c873a378-3273-5881-aef0-5637501c5e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373362} {:answer/id #uuid "305573ba-78aa-5688-a91d-84af92c44465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c873a378-3273-5881-aef0-5637501c5e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373362} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb208b6-24cb-5f43-8985-2eaf15597945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a56fdf8-4093-582a-8eb3-1fa47565653f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94311" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd81993a-d79b-58f4-854a-4c5ee4f579cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+7y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373366} {:answer/id #uuid "66496ea1-f2ed-50f4-9655-d1e677ef861e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373367} {:answer/id #uuid "0028312c-8e4d-5205-ba25-90628198a2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373368} {:answer/id #uuid "61ba141d-e63e-57e2-b6db-32f25c1bafa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373369} {:answer/id #uuid "d50b4733-17ac-5392-8969-541607d84e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0028312c-8e4d-5205-ba25-90628198a2c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373368} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb6d120-f8d3-5599-84ed-12974816e9c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "704b1e51-f6aa-5eaa-825a-515f3e83eae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94324" :question/problem "A particle of mass $14\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=28\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=14\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n14{y}^{″}=-14{y}^{′}-28y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a754ea-72be-5685-acda-795d9b8347ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373373} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd8fadb-cc4a-552f-8ebd-de912e53958a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373374} {:answer/id #uuid "254df75f-d9bf-5c29-a12d-8dfbfb4b2295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373375} {:answer/id #uuid "244ca7a2-2043-5ae4-929c-f91939d47b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373376} {:answer/id #uuid "feb062c5-a381-5673-820c-b0d292620888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244ca7a2-2043-5ae4-929c-f91939d47b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373376} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c128aa-89eb-5e48-95d3-7e6238a342dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2923e945-2143-52cb-b5fe-a0a714d9e55e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94359" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=1\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=2\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}]}
